WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:05.569
Tier lists have been a staple of YouTube for
a few years now, at this point, you can find

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:07.930
a ranking of just about anything.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:13.990
TierZoo was the first channel I saw to combine
the format with science and learning.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.260
But it's already spread into physics and maths
as well.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:22.080
For example, Andrew Dotson recently did a
ranking of his favourite physics equations.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.070
Today, I thought it would be time to create
an ultimate mathematical shapes tier list.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:35.210
I've got a non rigorous point system to give
each shape a fair chance at that coveted S-tier

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.210
position.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:43.510
So each object will be judged on symmetry,
singularities, the significance in other disciplines

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:50.720
outside of mathematics, how difficult it is
to define or create and the aesthetics.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.840
For aesthetics, we're really asking, does
this shape deserve its own mood board on Pinterest.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:02.820
Now, that's of course going to be subjective,
but it's worth including aesthetics as a criteria,

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.660
because it's kind of important to math.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.840
The beauty of the shape may indicate that
we've got some kind of elegant solution, or

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.280
at least to help inspire interest in the topic.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.170
So let's start with a nice simple shape, the
common sphere, I'll be using the software

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.860
SURFER to model some of these shapes.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.750
This algebraic surface is as old as it gets,
it is perfectly symmetric, which makes it

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:28.990
awesome to work with.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:35.120
But at the same time, it might be a bit boring
and uninspiring because it looks the same

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.590
in all directions.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:41.770
However, this hasn't stopped the sphere from
becoming a major player in other disciplines.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:46.130
You see it in art and sports and engineering
all the time.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:51.870
And even in astrophysics for things like planets,
and even black holes.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.390
Spheres are very effective for modelling all
sorts of things.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.500
Probably because the sphere is very easy to
understand.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.790
Aesthetics wise, the sphere might not be the
most interesting shape.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:08.739
But because of its heroic performance in all
sorts of disciplines, and how easy it is to

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:12.859
construct, I think that B-tier is more than
deserved.

00:02:12.859 --> 00:02:16.760
Next up is another classic, the Mobius strip.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.129
It's a two dimensional object with only one
side and one edge that can be embedded in

00:02:22.129 --> 00:02:23.749
a three dimensional space.

00:02:23.749 --> 00:02:28.989
There are no notable things to say about it
symmetry or singularities.

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:35.189
But just like with the sphere, where a mobius
strip shines is in its applications to other

00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:36.279
disciplines.

00:02:36.279 --> 00:02:41.239
Surprisingly, there are actually some applications
of this strange little shape.

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:47.620
One of them is that in the days of industrialization,
a lot of factories had one central engine

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:54.449
to provide rotational energy to operate all
the machines, a common thing was to use flat

00:02:54.449 --> 00:02:58.340
leather belts to transmit the central power.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:03.559
Some were instead given a half twist before
joining the ends and this would allow for

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:08.379
the wear to be evenly distributed over both
sides of the belt.

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:13.620
You can make a mobius strip pretty easily
yourself, just take a long piece of paper,

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.980
give it a twist and glue the ends together.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:22.579
If you start at any point and draw along the
strip, you end up we started.

00:03:22.579 --> 00:03:27.989
In mathematics, we call the shape with a property
like this non orientable.

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:31.979
Since you can't really define an up or down
on a surface like this.

00:03:31.979 --> 00:03:38.669
I think the simplicity of being able to create
such an interesting shape makes it very intriguing

00:03:38.669 --> 00:03:42.450
and worthy of an A-tier spot.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:48.890
Now this next crazy critter is the Barth Sextic,
which I recently made an entire video about.

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:54.659
It's going to belong in S-tier because it's
got very interesting symmetry, record breaking

00:03:54.659 --> 00:03:58.029
singularities and an intriguing history.

00:03:58.029 --> 00:04:03.409
It is one of the world record surfaces since
it holds the record for having the largest

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:11.139
number of singularities for a polynomial of
degree six, that record that it holds is 65

00:04:11.139 --> 00:04:12.409
singularities.

00:04:12.409 --> 00:04:18.690
And it was proven that it can never be beaten,
that 66 singularities isn't possible.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:23.419
Now there are some other members of the world
record squad and they ought to join it in

00:04:23.419 --> 00:04:24.590
here as well.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:32.229
So we have the Togliatti Quintic, which holds
the record for a polynomial of degree five,

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:34.759
and that record is 31 singularities.

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:42.189
I think they belong one tier below the Barth
Sextic since the record is for a lower degree.

00:04:42.189 --> 00:04:48.000
Then Likewise, we have the Kummer Quartic,
which holds the record for a polynomial of

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.110
degree four.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:53.389
And that record it holds is 16 singularities.

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:59.659
I'll put that one in B-tier, and then we'll
have the Kayley Cubic, another world record

00:04:59.659 --> 00:05:06.169
surface, this time of degree three, coming
in with four singularities, and getting placed

00:05:06.169 --> 00:05:07.169
in C-tier.

00:05:07.169 --> 00:05:13.210
The next one is also a record holder, it's
the double cone coming in with one singularity,

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.210
for degree two.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.919
I talked about this one a bit as well in my
video about the Barth Sextic, you can really

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:22.759
see that prominent singularity in the middle
of the shape.

00:05:22.759 --> 00:05:28.820
This shape is also quite interesting in special
relativity in physics, as you might have seen

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.090
it in something called a light cone.

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:35.360
It shows the causal future and the past.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.419
You can think of the singularity in the middle
as an event in space time, and all other events

00:05:41.419 --> 00:05:48.669
inside the double cone can have a causal relationship
with that first event, everything outside

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:55.669
the cone would be too far away to have any
impact, or to be impacted by the original

00:05:55.669 --> 00:05:57.110
event in space time.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:01.300
And this angle of the cone is governed by
the speed of light.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:06.009
That's a very interesting application but
because the record this one holds is not as

00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:10.810
impressive as some of the other records we've
seen, it's going to sit in C-tier.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:15.819
And now I've got two more shapes to quickly
mention here, which also currently hold world

00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:19.169
records but perhaps not for long.

00:06:19.169 --> 00:06:22.870
These are the Labs-Septic, and the Endrass-Octic.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:28.200
These currently hold records for seven and
eighth degree polynomials.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:34.289
Although these records haven't been proven
to be unbeatable in the way that our other

00:06:34.289 --> 00:06:35.310
record holders were.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:41.030
For the Barth Sextic, we've proven that you
can never have more than 65 singularities.

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:47.550
But here for the septic, and this optic, it's
very feasible that someone might be able to

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.210
find a shape which will beat it.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:53.120
For that reason, they're both going to be
banished to D-tier.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.749
Let's see what we can do next, we have Gauss
Bell surface.

00:06:57.749 --> 00:07:03.539
The surface might look unspectacular at first,
since there are no interesting points that

00:07:03.539 --> 00:07:06.250
really stand out, no singularities.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:09.939
But don't underestimate this gem of mathematics.

00:07:09.939 --> 00:07:15.949
It is used all the time in statistics and
is the foundation of the normal distribution.

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:22.090
Furthermore, what's very interesting is that
the formula defining the surface shows a really

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:27.699
nice connection between the fundamental constants
pi and e.

00:07:27.699 --> 00:07:32.330
This one is difficult to rank because the
aesthetics are lacking a bit and the competition

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:35.779
is harsh, but we'll place them here in C-tier.

00:07:35.779 --> 00:07:41.550
Next up, we can do the Torus, or you might
like to call it a doughnut.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:48.259
It is a rotational symmetric shape of genus
one, since it has one hole, and this shape

00:07:48.259 --> 00:07:53.069
is often brought up when talking about the
topic of topology.

00:07:53.069 --> 00:07:58.699
Everything with one hole is said to be topologically
equivalent to a Torus, and the classic example

00:07:58.699 --> 00:08:02.400
is a coffee mug with a handle or drinking
straw.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:09.099
Of course, its applications are very wide
from hair ties, to rings, to edible donuts,

00:08:09.099 --> 00:08:11.189
or even pool toys.

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:16.430
It has vast applications and because of that,
we're going to put it in B-tier.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:22.900
Next we have a shape which is a close relative
of one that we've already seen.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:30.220
This is the Klein bottle, and it's very similar
to the Mobius strip since both surfaces are

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.419
non orientable.

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:38.510
The Klein bottle also has only one surface
but unlike the strip, the two dimensional

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:44.850
bottle can't be embedded in three dimensions,
it always looks like it intersects itself,

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:47.200
when in reality, it doesn't.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:51.070
You might have noticed this shape in my profile
picture here on YouTube.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:57.070
The beanie that I wear is actually a Klein
bottle hat that I made by knitting it.

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:04.120
Although of course, it's not an accurate Klein
bottle since that can't exist in 3D.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:10.610
If you see the way it intersects, that wouldn't
happen if we had the luxury of a fourth spatial

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:11.709
dimension.

00:09:11.709 --> 00:09:17.570
Unfortunately, making nice hats and glass
bottles might be the limit of applications

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:21.660
that I know about the Klein bottle to the
world outside mathematics.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:27.459
But the Klein bottle definitely deserves to
be ranked alongside the Mobius strip up here

00:09:27.459 --> 00:09:28.990
and A-tier.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:34.680
Especially because if you actually were to
split a Klein bottle in half, what you would

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.410
have would be two Mobius strips.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:44.350
This little twisty boy is called the Trefoil
knot qnd it does seem to be the poster child

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:45.350
for knot theory.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:54.230
It is named after the three leaf clover, the
Trefoil plant and it has some nice triangular

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:55.730
sort of symmetry to it.

00:09:55.730 --> 00:10:01.250
The knot is one continuous loop and easy enough
to make yourself using a string.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:06.480
I also happened to have this sculpture lying
around, which is another example of this kind

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:07.480
of knot.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.630
So clearly, it's gonna rank pretty high on
aesthetics, if they're literally selling it

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:12.750
as a decoration.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:18.320
I don't have a great deal of knowledge about
knot theory but apparently this is the first

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.399
non trivial knot.

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:25.190
And one of the reasons why knot theory is
so interesting is that it seems to have many

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:31.560
applications in biology and chemistry with
things like proteins and DNA that are knotted

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.560
in some sense.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.820
I'm going to rank it in B-tier for now, it's
got some points for aesthetics, some points

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:43.269
for a possible usefulness even though I might
not totally understand how this note is useful,

00:10:43.269 --> 00:10:46.100
but it is still a fairly basic shape.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:50.800
We're back to a heavy hitter with the next
one, Flamm's paraboloid.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.410
This one's going to get ranked right up at
S-tier.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:59.519
It doesn't perhaps look that spectacular,
but it is pretty cool.

00:10:59.519 --> 00:11:05.459
This shape mimics the outside of a black hole
in the space time called the Schwarzschild

00:11:05.459 --> 00:11:06.459
solution.

00:11:06.459 --> 00:11:12.889
In 1915 when Einstein published his famous
field equations of general relativity, he

00:11:12.889 --> 00:11:15.130
didn't actually solve them himself.

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:22.759
Instead, a German scientist named Karl Schwarzschild
found the first interesting solution in relativity,

00:11:22.759 --> 00:11:26.079
a spacetime containing a black hole.

00:11:26.079 --> 00:11:31.399
He calculated this while he was stationed
in Russia during the First World War.

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:35.910
And sadly, he died not long after his groundbreaking
discovery.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:42.760
He calculated that spacetime stops behaving
normal at some radius, this is the inner circle

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:46.949
in the middle, now, we call this the event
horizon.

00:11:46.949 --> 00:11:52.819
The surface shows how everything gets attracted
to that circle, and what happens beyond this

00:11:52.819 --> 00:11:56.199
point is anyone's guess at the moment.

00:11:56.199 --> 00:12:02.120
It is amazing to see how clearly general relativity
predicted the existence of black holes like

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:05.839
this, when no one could imagine this before.

00:12:05.839 --> 00:12:11.900
This is a cornerstone of science wrapped up
in one picture and that's why it deserves

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:13.970
to be in S-tier.

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:17.339
Second to last, here we have the dodecahedron.

00:12:17.339 --> 00:12:23.160
Now, this is supposed to represent all five
of the platonic solids.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:27.850
We've all seen them before, and they're so
iconic that they definitely deserve to be

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:28.850
ranked here.

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:32.621
All of these solids have some very special
symmetry to them.

00:12:32.621 --> 00:12:38.889
And in fact, these solids define what symmetries
other shapes can have.

00:12:38.889 --> 00:12:45.160
For example, back in my Barth Sextic video,
it was the symmetry of the icosahedron that

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.579
allowed us to get that shape.

00:12:47.579 --> 00:12:52.810
What makes the platonic solids so special
is the uniqueness they have.

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:59.779
These shapes are the only options if you want
a polyhedron with identical faces and corners.

00:12:59.779 --> 00:13:03.850
And the fact that there are only five seems
quite surprising.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:09.269
You see these shapes, of course, a lot in
architecture, or anywhere really throughout

00:13:09.269 --> 00:13:10.329
history.

00:13:10.329 --> 00:13:14.509
And so considering this A-tier is a fitting
spot.

00:13:14.509 --> 00:13:19.170
And this list would of course, not be complete
without at least one fractal.

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:22.670
I've chosen to include the Mandelbrot set.

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:29.449
This relatively new shape finds its home in
complex analysis, and is known for its crazy

00:13:29.449 --> 00:13:30.690
boundary.

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:34.750
It is one of those fractals which are inherently
self similar.

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:39.190
Meaning that you can zoom in on the border
as much as you want and would always end up

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:41.329
with the same thing on screen.

00:13:41.329 --> 00:13:46.820
There isn't too much to talk about in terms
of symmetry, and singularities with this one.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:52.810
And I'm not sure about any usefulness in other
scientific fields, but it is used a lot in

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:54.010
art.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:59.250
And as I'm sure you can imagine, or might
have seen, there are plenty of nice, psychedelic

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:04.520
like zooming videos using this shape, plenty
of which you can watch here on YouTube.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:08.069
The construction of this shape is surprisingly
simple.

00:14:08.069 --> 00:14:13.100
And I'm going to explore it using Brilliant
who are also a sponsor of this video and have

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:15.350
been a huge supporter of my channel.

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:20.209
I do really like their explanation and a lot
of their visuals for the Mandelbrot set.

00:14:20.209 --> 00:14:26.100
So let's have a look at how they say we can
define it, what you do is you start with a

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:33.009
complex number, call it z, then if you square
that and add it to itself, look at what you

00:14:33.009 --> 00:14:40.279
end up with, then square that and add z to
that and keep going like that forever.

00:14:40.279 --> 00:14:46.130
You'll notice that one of two things would
happen; either the magnitude explodes up towards

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:52.300
infinity, and in that case, the complex number
z is not in the Mandelbrot set.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:58.230
If the magnitude is bounded or tends towards
zero, then the numbers z is in the Mandelbrot

00:14:58.230 --> 00:14:59.230
set.

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:02.899
We can ask ourselves is the complex number
i in the set?

00:15:02.899 --> 00:15:10.601
Well, it is because after the first few iterations,
we would have i, then minus one plus i, then

00:15:10.601 --> 00:15:14.819
minus i, then back to minus one plus i.

00:15:14.819 --> 00:15:19.339
And this set of answers will repeat, we're
in a bit of an alternating cycle.

00:15:19.339 --> 00:15:25.410
And since we're not going to end up going
out to infinity, the value of i is in the

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:30.279
Mandelbrot set, so it will be plotted somewhere
on that interesting picture.

00:15:30.279 --> 00:15:33.420
Another example is the real number one.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:39.370
With that we would have one, then two, then
five, then 26.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:43.980
And because each time we're squaring that
answer and adding one to it, we're just going

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:47.779
to keep getting larger and larger until we
get to infinity.

00:15:47.779 --> 00:15:51.220
So one is not in the Mandelbrot set.

00:15:51.220 --> 00:15:57.180
Another couple of examples here we have that
minus two is in the Mandelbrot set, whilst

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:00.029
minus two i is not.

00:16:00.029 --> 00:16:06.240
This here is a full image of the Mandelbrot
set and you can zoom in on parts of the boundary

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.069
to see its chaotic nature.

00:16:08.069 --> 00:16:15.329
The set has a main cardioid, which is this
heart shaped section here, as well as several

00:16:15.329 --> 00:16:17.290
attached bulbs.

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:22.740
And the position of a number within either
the cardioid, or these numbered bulbs here

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:27.850
tells us something about recursion, or I think
the fate of the number, whether it reaches

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.970
an equilibrium or a fixed point.

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:36.329
I love looking at this fractal because every
piece of it is loaded with mathematical meaning.

00:16:36.329 --> 00:16:41.189
And one of the reasons that it might be so
appealing is because you get such a complicated

00:16:41.189 --> 00:16:45.899
surface with a relatively easy mathematical
construction.

00:16:45.899 --> 00:16:51.069
To this date, there are still plenty of open
questions surrounding the set and it has led

00:16:51.069 --> 00:16:58.800
to the discovery of many equally astounding
shapes, such as the Mandelbulb, or the Buddhabrot.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.839
It is pretty legendary so I'm going to put
it up in S-tier.

00:17:01.839 --> 00:17:04.420
Let me know what you think of my ranking.

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:08.410
I'm sure there will be some that you disagree
with but it's just for fun.

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:12.420
And I'm sure there's also some interesting
shapes that I've left out, because how could

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:17.780
I include all of them in one video, but tell
me some of those main ones you think should

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:19.210
be included next time.

